  parts:[
    p('Keep the owner until the release is confirmed','The technician’s Stop action tells us the answer is no longer wanted. It does not yet tell us the worker has finished cleaning up. We mark that request cancel_requested and continue counting its reservation. Only the execution layer’s confirmed release lets us give the same storage to another request.\n\nOur fixture calls that final state released. A real runtime may expose task completion, an abort acknowledgement or a different lifecycle event. Handle races with ordinary completion and repeated cancellation requests deliberately.',null,'The state names are a teaching interface. The property we need is an authoritative end to resource ownership.'),
    p('A waiting queue needs a limit of its own','Requests that cannot start yet may wait in a queue. Accepting more of them still uses memory and can make every waiting person wait longer. Backpressure means passing that capacity problem back toward the sender: the service may ask it to slow down, or explicitly refuse work it cannot accept. Our model uses a limit on the number of waiting requests.\n\nA queue-size limit bounds accepted waiting items in this model. It does not guarantee their waiting time, because service durations and arrivals can still vary.', 'waiting limit = 2 requests\nalready waiting = [Q1]\nnew arrivals = [Q2,Q3]\nqueue after admission = [Q1,Q2]\nexplicitly rejected arrivals = [Q3]', 'A refusal is observable work the service did not accept. Do not remove it from load reports and call the remainder the whole workload.'),
    p('Cancelling a stream does not cancel an earlier external effect','If an agent already submitted an inspection booking, ending text generation cannot unsend that booking. A timed-out reply might also conceal a committed tool result. Keep effect outcomes and inference-resource cleanup as different records.\n\nA booking tool needs an identifier for the intended operation so that a retry can be recognised instead of making another booking. If the result is uncertain, the caller needs a way to check what happened. Those are the effect-handling problems from the earlier agent lessons. This workshop confines itself to resource ownership and the waiting queue.',null,'A successful cancellation acknowledgement here says nothing about whether a previous tool write occurred.'),
    p('Use deadlines without restarting the timer','A queued request can become useless before it reaches a worker. Propagate the original overall deadline, preserve response time, and avoid launching work when no useful allowance remains. An immediate retry loop after a capacity refusal can make overload worse.\n\nAn upstream producer needs a bounded retry policy appropriate to the operation, and a user may need a clear busy or cancelled outcome. The earlier deadline calculator supplies one component; it is not a complete queue scheduler.',null,'The deadline answers when work stops being useful; the queue limit answers how much waiting work we accept.'),
    p('Test cancellation while the system is already full','Exercise cancellation before dispatch, during decoding and after ordinary completion. Check that cleanup happens once, that retained work remains counted, and that later requests do not inherit another request’s cache state.\n\nMeasure abandoned requests and confirmed releases separately. If they differ, the service may still be doing work no caller wants. The simulation cannot establish how promptly a deployed worker actually cleans up.',null,'Inspect the release event beside its request ID so slow cleanup remains visible.'),
  ],
